  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhancing the Protection of Crisis-Affected Mobile Populations in Indonesia, Thailand, and Malaysia through the Provision of Life-Saving Assistance and Multi-Stakeholder Advocacy at the Regional and Local Levels</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Complex mobility flows remain a dominant feature in the Southeast Asia region, stemming from the multi-dimensional crisis in Myanmar. The systematic human rights violations against the Rohingya people and the February 2021 military takeover in Myanmar have forced people to flee the country and seek refuge in neighbouring countries including Malaysia, Indonesia and Thailand. This, combined with the lack of sustainable solutions and reduction in assistance provided in Cox&apos;s Bazaar, has led to an increase in maritime and land movements from Bangladesh and Myanmar to Malaysia, Indonesia and Thailand. This action contributes to overall efforts to enhance protection of refugees and migrants, including Rohingya refugees, and other conflict affected populations undertaking maritime and land movements in Indonesia, Thailand and Malaysia. The International Organization for Migration (IOM) is providing multi-sectoral assistance that is people-centred and context specific to over 1,850 refugees. Sectoral assistance includes Health, Food, Non-Food Items, Water, Sanitation and Hygiene, Shelter, Protection and multi-purpose cash grants to new arrivals and residual caseloads. IOM also continues to gather, analyze and share data on complex mobility in the region with a particular focus on land and maritime movements of Rohingya refugees and cross-border mobility between Myanmar and Thailand.</narrative>
  </description>
